| 4 | Credit it and test it with users. *Whose work is in our program, and does it work for the people it is meant for?* Anchor: the team's own program, with a borrowed function, a library, an image from the web and a saved generated snippet in it, and no credits | 8 | g12-attribution; g12-refine-with-users | HS-PRO-PD-14; HS-PRO-TR-20; S1-SWD-UX-05 | Concept area: programming (program development, testing and refining). Practices: ESR2 (credit others' work); HCD10 (learn from users outside the team); HCD11 (test and refine for use and access); HCD12 (users informed and in control); CT9 (change one thing, test again); IC3 (give and take feedback) | Day 1 (understanding): attribution on a supplied program first: each library, each piece of others' code, each image or sound and any generated code gets a credits line (creator, source, license) and a note on the permission it needs, from supplied license cards (a permissive license, a share-alike license, all rights reserved, public domain) (HS-PRO-PD-14). Day 2 (device or paper): the team writes the credits section of its own program. Day 3: the intended users and setting stated, then a heuristic review of the team's program against a short list (usable, consistent, accessible, user in control), asking how it serves users of different abilities, languages and cultures; worked first on a supplied screen; the recommendations it yields (S1-SWD-UX-05). Day 4: user round one: two users from another team try the program and say what they see and what confuses them; the team records the testing results. Day 5: the team changes one thing at a time (a help command, plainer instructions, text size and contrast, a fixed error) and re-checks each against the test-table and the values in its design document (HS-PRO-TR-20). Day 6: user round two. Day 7: the last changes re-checked; a refinement record (what users and tests showed, what changed, why it serves the intended users better). Day 8: the final program, credits and refinement record handed in (recorded, not scored); pairs practice on mixed retrieval items from units 1 to 3. Out: copyright law, statute text, a survey, fixing every issue, accessibility work beyond established guidelines (the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines named as the guide teams apply). End-of-unit: the year-end course-level check (day 55) stands for it. Tools: our own editor, the heuristic review checklist, the design-document and log template. Curio Class writes: the supplied uncredited program and its key, the license cards, the supplied screen for the heuristic review, the user-round note sheets | After unit 3 because both rows act on the finished team program (row notes); attribution first so the program users try is the credited one, and the heuristic review before the user rounds so the team knows its intended users before it listens to them. Ends in week 27, so the year-end check in week 28 can stand for it | Candidate: kept, an extra user round for a team whose second round still shows a blocking fault, folded into day 7, zero added days | Users of different abilities, languages and cultures are studied through the checklist and the users in the room, never by asking a student about their own disability or home language; a student may offer. Users from another team take part by choice and may decline. Agent reads: sensitivity (the W3C page in Sensitivity reads first). |
| 5 | What our team taught us, and where I go next. *How do different people's views make computing better, and where could what I have learned take me?* Anchor: two entries from the team's own log, the day a teammate's question changed the design | 8 | g12-diverse-teams; g12-career-goals | HS-SOC-CE-45; HS-SOC-CE-46 | Concept area: computing and society (career exploration). Practices: IC5 (reflect on the team's work); HCD10 (different views and users); CT6 (frame a personal goal); IC3 (feedback on a draft); ESR1 (a more inclusive field) | Day 1: the hook and the question. Day 2: the team's log studied for two moments where a different view changed a decision, and what the team would have lost without it, laid on the benefit-risk-chart (evidence from the log, never opinion about people). Day 3: two first-person published accounts by computing professionals from different backgrounds: Grace Hopper's oral history interview in the Smithsonian's National Museum of American History Computer Oral History Collection (1968, americanhistory.si.edu, archives center; from memory, checked at source) and Katherine Johnson's account in NASA's published interviews and biography (nasa.gov); how each used computational thinking at work, met a barrier and opened the field to others; students draft their analysis (HS-SOC-CE-45). Picks checked against the grade 6 to 11 rows, which name neither. Day 4: the student's portfolio read for two skills shown in this course, with evidence, and two computing-related fields researched from supplied field cards built from the Bureau of Labor Statistics' Occupational Outlook Handbook, computer and information technology group (bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/, duties only). Day 5: the plan drafted: a goal, two skills with evidence, one pathway to check (a certificate, an apprenticeship, a two-year or four-year program, military service, work), one next step (HS-SOC-CE-46). Day 6: the teacher's written feedback on the analysis and the plan returned; both revised. Out: a final career choice, an internship, an interview with a professional, formal research, comparing schools or pay. End-of-unit (day 7): the revised analysis (HS-SOC-CE-45) and plan (HS-SOC-CE-46) handed in and scored, the plan read privately, with a short unseen item: a new team log excerpt in which students name where a different view changed a decision; respond and close day 8. Short form if seniors leave before week 31: Grade 12 calendar, above. Tools: the design-document and log template (the team's log). Curio Class writes: the reading guides for the two accounts, the field and pathway cards, the plan sheet, the unseen log excerpt | Last because both rows read the year's own work: CE-45's log comes from unit 3's workflow (the row's in-grade order) and CE-46's portfolio from units 1 to 4. Its rows hand on nothing, so it can follow the year-end check in week 28, and its short form keeps both ids met for seniors who leave in weeks 31 and 32 | Candidate: dropped, a visit by a computing professional; the standard asks for published accounts, not interviews, and a visit needs a school's own arrangement, zero days | The accounts show race and gender barriers at work: told as the professional tells them, with respect; no student is asked whether they face such barriers. A plan can touch family money, immigration status or a family's expectations: the plan is private by default, shared only at the level of ideas, and no pathway is ranked above another; the teacher page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps. A student 18 or over makes their own leave-out choice. Agent reads: sensitivity (starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |

## Course-level checks

