ample. Day 6 (unplugged): version history on paper branch cards: branch, review, resolve a clash between two changes, merge (S1-SWD-PP-08; part met for the version control part, see Devices). Day 7 (modify): each pair changes a supplied starter module to fit its design and tests it. Days 8 to 15 (make): pairs build their modules, driver and navigator switching at the halfway signal, a log entry each day; review steps on days 11 and 15, each a teammate reading code against the design document. Day 16: modules joined and the whole team test-table run. Day 17: the draft program and design document handed in. Day 18: the teacher's written feedback against the rubric returned; teams plan fixes. Day 19: revision and a final test-table run; the program is scored after this day. Day 20: each team presents its program with a trace of its key part, each member presenting their own module. Out: a professional project tool, a version-control server, large projects, any personal data in a program, any live generated-text tool. End-of-unit: the team program scored after days 18 and 19, each member's part read from the log, and day 21, an individual sitting with unseen items: find the workflow fault in a new team's design document and log, trace a short given module, and sign off or return a new submission with a reason (HS-PRO-PD-15; S1-SWD-PP-08's review and inclusive norms); respond day 22. Tools: our own editor (Python, step-through trace), the design-document and log template; the editor's version history when built. Curio Class writes: the worked team project with its documents and log, the starter modules for each menu project with tested solutions, the right and flawed submissions, the branch cards, the rubric with a meeting and a non-meeting sample | After units 1 and 2 because the design document records whose values shaped each decision (unit 2) and the team program is the one units 4 and 5 read. The workflow and the capstone are one unit, since the capstone is the team